---
concept: unmodeled
---
-- replay --
$ dorc explain --terse unmodeled
A command is unmodeled when no loaded oracle describes it. Dorc treats
it as capable of changing anything on the host, because in Dorc, silence
never grants permission.

$ dorc explain --summary unmodeled
Dorc never guesses what a command does. Everything it knows arrives as
description: an oracle, written by a person, that models a command's
checks and effects in runnable shell. A command with no oracle loaded is
unmodeled, and the safe reading of no-description is any-effect: the
command may change anything on the host, so nothing may be skipped,
guarded, or reasoned past on its account. Unmodeled is the normal
starting state of every command in the world; description is how a
command graduates out of it, and one small oracle at a time is how a
book does.

$ dorc explain unmodeled
unmodeled

